' Pattern component notes
note top of CommissionIterator
  <b>Iterator Interface</b>

  Defines uniform traversal interface:
  • hasNext() - check if more elements
  • next() - get next element
  • remaining() - count left
  • reset() - restart iteration

  <b>Key principle:</b>
  All iterators implement same interface,
  enabling polymorphic iteration.
end note

note right of AllDealsIterator
  <b>Simple Iterator</b>

  Forward iteration through all deals:
  • Maintains currentIndex
  • No filtering logic
  • Direct array access

  <b>Use case:</b>
  When all deals needed in order.
end note

note right of StatusFilterIterator
  <b>Filtering Iterator</b>

  Iterates only deals with target status:
  • Lazy evaluation (finds on demand)
  • Caches next element
  • Skips non-matching deals

  <b>Use case:</b>
  Process won deals, open deals, etc.

  <b>Implementation:</b>
  findNext() searches for next match.
end note

note right of ValueRangeIterator
  <b>Range Filter Iterator</b>

  Filters deals by value range:
  • min/max threshold checking
  • Lazy evaluation
  • Supports complex criteria

  <b>Use case:</b>
  High-value pipeline, small deals,
  specific value segments.
end note

note right of ValueSortedIterator
  <b>Sorted Iterator</b>

  Provides sorted traversal:
  • Creates sorted copy on construction
  • Sorted by value (descending)
  • Independent of original order

  <b>Use case:</b>
  Top deals report, priority processing.

  <b>Note:</b>
  Snapshot approach - safe from
  concurrent modifications.
end note

note left of DealCollection
  <b>Aggregate (Collection)</b>

  Stores deals and provides iterators:
  • Hides internal structure
  • Factory methods for iterators
  • Multiple iterator types

  <b>Iterator Factory Methods:</b>
  • createIterator() - all deals
  • createStatusIterator() - by status
  • createValueRangeIterator() - by value
  • createDateIterator() - by date
  • createValueSortedIterator() - sorted

  <b>Key principle:</b>
  Collection structure completely
  hidden from clients.
end note

note left of CommissionPlanRepository
  <b>Plan Aggregate</b>

  Specialized collection for plans:
  • Active plans only
  • Effective on specific date
  • Different domain, same pattern

  <b>Demonstrates:</b>
  Iterator pattern applies to
  any collection type.
end note

note bottom of CompositeIterator
  <b>Composite Iterator</b>

  Combines multiple iterators:
  • Traverses multiple collections
  • Seamless transition between iterators
  • Useful for federated data

  <b>Use case:</b>
  Aggregate data from multiple sources,
  process related collections together.
end note

' Usage flow diagram
note as N1
  <b>TYPICAL USAGE FLOW</b>

  <b>1. Create Iterator:</b>
     Client → DealCollection.createStatusIterator(WON)
       → Returns StatusFilterIterator instance
       → Iterator holds reference to collection

  <b>2. Check for Elements:</b>
     Client → iterator.hasNext()
       → Iterator.findNext() searches for match
       → Caches result for efficiency
       → Returns true/false

  <b>3. Get Next Element:</b>
     Client → iterator.next()
       → Returns cached element
       → Advances position
       → Ready for next hasNext()

  <b>4. Repeat 2-3:</b>
     Continue until hasNext() returns false

  <b>Key Points:</b>
  • Client only uses iterator interface
  • Internal structure completely hidden
  • Filtering logic in iterator
  • Collection unchanged during iteration
end note

' Iterator strategies comparison
note as N2
  <b>ITERATOR STRATEGIES</b>

  <b>All Deals Iterator:</b>
  ┌─────────────────────────┐
  │ Deal1 → Deal2 → Deal3   │
  │   ↓       ↓       ↓     │
  │  ALL    ALL    ALL      │
  └─────────────────────────┘
  Processes every deal.

  <b>Status Filter Iterator:</b>
  ┌─────────────────────────┐
  │ Deal1 → Deal2 → Deal3   │
  │ (OPEN)  (WON)  (LOST)   │
  │   ✗       ✓      ✗      │
  └─────────────────────────┘
  Only returns Deal2 (WON).

  <b>Value Range Iterator:</b>
  ┌─────────────────────────┐
  │ $5k  →  $50k  →  $10k   │
  │  ✗       ✓       ✓      │
  └─────────────────────────┘
  Returns deals in $10k-$100k range.

  <b>Sorted Iterator:</b>
  ┌─────────────────────────┐
  │ Original: 1, 3, 2       │
  │ Sorted:   3, 2, 1       │
  └─────────────────────────┘
  Creates sorted copy, returns in order.

  Same collection, different views!
end note

' Legend
legend right
  <b>Iterator Pattern Components:</b>

  <b>Iterator (Interface):</b> Defines traversal
  interface. All iterators implement this,
  enabling polymorphic iteration.

  <b>ConcreteIterator:</b> Implements specific
  traversal strategy. Maintains current
  position and filtering logic.

  <b>Aggregate:</b> Collection that provides
  iterator factory methods. Hides internal
  structure from clients.

  <b>Key Benefits:</b>
  ✓ Encapsulation (internals hidden)
  ✓ Uniform interface (hasNext/next)
  ✓ Multiple simultaneous traversals
  ✓ Flexible filtering strategies
  ✓ Easy to add new iterator types
  ✓ Separation of concerns

  <b>Traversal Encapsulation:</b>
  Client code independent of how collection
  stores data. Can change from List to Array
  to Tree without affecting clients.

  <b>Multiple Strategies:</b>
  Same collection can be traversed in
  different ways: filtered, sorted, reversed,
  skipped, etc. Each iterator encapsulates
  its strategy.
endlegend

@enduml